+# R300 prototype tests
+
+Run `tests/poweremu/run-r300-metal-test.sh` on a Mac with Metal. It builds in a temporary directory, runs on the real host GPU, then removes its executable. No VM or guest disk is opened.
+
+The test translates native R300 **fragment** instruction words into MSL. A fixed host vertex shader provides triangle geometry; guest vertex ISA, vertex fetch, textures, float24 constant upload and guest VRAM presentation are not implemented by this test. It is a decoder/backend building block, not a functioning Radeon 9800 device.
+
+Five positive cases verify 3,644 non-edge pixels each against independent expected colors and triangle coverage, using a private RGBA32Float render target, explicit GPU-to-shared-buffer blit, command completion and CPU readback. They cover:
+
+- Constant color/alpha MAD.
+- Independent RGB/alpha source banks and cross-channel selection.
+- Nontrivial multiplication/addition without implicit output clamping.
+- Explicit saturation.
+- Constant index 31, swizzle, absolute value and negation order.
+
+Negative cases require rejection of textures, multiple instructions, depth output, unsupported ALU operations, temporary inputs/outputs, nonzero output index, unverified half operands, and undersized MSL output buffers. Unsupported programs must never silently become a different shader.
+
+The compiler supports only one texture-free ALU node containing one constant-source MAD and full output 0. The caller supplies already-decoded float32 constants. The experimental QEMU device calls it only to classify captured shader state; it still rejects guest 3D draws.
